Sec. 20001 appropriates $8.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through FY2029) to improve military quality of life, including $2.9 billion to supplement Basic Allowance for Housing, $2 billion for the Defense Health Program, $1 billion for unaccompanied housing sustainment, and $590 million to extend the Temporary Lodging Expense Allowance to 21 days. It also temporarily raises the maximum private investment in certain privatized military housing projects to 60 percent (from 33⅓ and 45 percent) through September 30, 2029, and amends 10 U.S.C. 2881a to authorize the Secretary concerned to acquire or construct privatized military unaccompanied housing, extending that authority to September 30, 2029.

Sec. 20002 appropriates $25.6 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) for shipbuilding, including $5.4 billion for two additional DDG ships, $4.6 billion for a second Virginia-class submarine in FY2026, $2.725 billion for T-AO oilers, $2.1 billion for medium unmanned surface vessels, and $1.8 billion for Landing Ship Medium procurement.

Sec. 20003 appropriates funds to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) for integrated air and missile defense, including $7.2 billion for military space-based sensors, $5.6 billion for space-based and boost-phase intercept, $2.55 billion for missile defense capabilities, $2.2 billion to accelerate hypersonic defense, and $1.975 billion for improved ground-based missile defense radars.

Sec. 20004 appropriates funds to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) for munitions and supply chain resiliency, including $2 billion for the National Defense Stockpile critical minerals fund, $1 billion each for one-way attack UAV industrial base expansion and next-generation automated munitions factories, and $500 million each for maritime mines and counter-UAS programs. It also provides $3.3 billion for Industrial Base Fund grants, $5 billion for critical minerals supply chain investments, and $500 million for the DoD Credit Program Account to support up to $100 billion in loans for critical minerals.

Sec. 20005 appropriates funds to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) to scale low-cost weapons, including $2 billion for Defense Innovation Unit commercial technology scaling, $1.5 billion for low-cost cruise missiles, $1.4 billion for small UAS industrial base expansion, and $1 billion each for qualification activities and innovative technology procurement. It also provides $1 billion to the DoD Credit Program Account to support up to $100 billion in loans under 10 U.S.C. 149(e).

Sec. 20006 appropriates $380 million to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) to improve DoD efficiency and cybersecurity, including $200 million for automation and AI to accelerate financial statement audits, $150 million for business systems replacement, $20 million for DARPA cybersecurity, and $10 million for the Office of the Secretary of Defense budgetary infrastructure.

Sec. 20007 appropriates funds to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) for air superiority, including $3.15 billion to increase F-15EX production, $750 million to accelerate the FA/XX aircraft, $678 million for the Collaborative Combat Aircraft program, $600 million for Air Force long-range strike aircraft, and $500 million for Navy long-range strike aircraft.

Sec. 20008 appropriates $10.7 billion to the Secretary of Defense and $3.885 billion to the NNSA Administrator for FY2025 (through FY2029) for nuclear forces, including $4.5 billion to expand B-21 production capacity, $2.5 billion for Sentinel ICBM risk reduction, $2 billion for the nuclear-armed sea-launched cruise missile, and $1 billion to accelerate NNSA facility construction.

Sec. 20009 appropriates funds to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) to improve U.S. Indo-Pacific Command capabilities, including $3.65 billion for military satellites, $1.1 billion for infrastructure development, $1 billion each for offensive cyber operations and the X-37B spacecraft program, and $850 million for replenishment of military articles.

Sec. 20010 appropriates funds to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) to improve readiness, including $2.5 billion for Air Force facilities sustainment, $2.118 billion for Air Force aircraft spares and repairs, $2 billion for Navy depot and shipyard modernization, $1.64 billion for Special Operations Command equipment, and $1.5 billion each for Army depot modernization and increased depot/shipyard maintenance.

Sec. 20011 appropriates $1 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (through FY2029) for military personnel deployment in support of border operations, operations and maintenance for border operations, counter-narcotics and counter-transnational criminal organization mission support, operation of national defense areas, and temporary detention of migrants on DoD installations under 10 U.S.C. chapter 15.

Sec. 20012 appropriates $10 million to the DoD Inspector General for FY2025 (through FY2029) to monitor DoD activities funded in this title, including programs with mutual technological dependencies, related data management and ownership considerations, and programs vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and long lead time components.

Sec. 20013 authorizes appropriations for military construction, land acquisition, and military family housing for each military department, and requires each Service Secretary to submit a detailed project-by-project spending plan to the Senate and House Armed Services Committees within 30 days of enactment. [end of text]


